# 2026-09-13 12:51:23.977862
+# AIDER PROMPT: De Bruijn Micro-Assembly Engine for Breakpoint Junction Reconstruction
+
+Add a local De Bruijn micro-assembly module in `src/retro_miner/local_assembly.py` to reconstruct full-length MEI insertion junction contigs from soft-clipped read fragments at candidate loci.
+
+LITERATURE ANCHOR:
+Cameron et al. (2017), "GRIDSS: sensitive and specific genomic rearrangement detection using positional de Bruijn graph assembly", Genome Research, 27(12):2050–2060.
+
+REQUIREMENTS:
+
+1. Core Assembly Engine (`src/retro_miner/local_assembly.py`):
+   - Implement `build_kmer_graph(sequences: list[str], k: int = 21) -> dict[str, list[str]]`:
+     * Extract $k$-mers and directed edges ($k\text{-mer}_{1 \dots k-1} \to k\text{-mer}_{2 \dots k}$) from all non-empty soft-clipped sequences.
+     * Prune $k$-mers with observation counts below coverage threshold ($C < 2$) to eliminate random sequencing errors.
+   - Implement `assemble_locus_clips(sequences: list[str], k: int = 21, min_coverage: int = 2) -> list[str]`:
+     * Traverse non-branching paths in the $k$-mer graph to form linear unitigs (assembled contigs).
+     * Return assembled unitigs ordered by length and coverage support.
+
+2. Unit Test Suite (`tests/test_vcf_export.py` or `tests/test_local_assembly.py`):
+   - Test synthetic overlapping soft-clips (`"ATTGCAGCTAGCTAG"`, `"AGCTAGCTAGAAAAA"`) assembling into a single 23-bp contig `"ATTGCAGCTAGCTAGAAAAA"`.
+   - Verify low-coverage noise filtering where single-ton $k$-mers are discarded.
+
+RUN COMMANDS:
+- pytest -v tests/test_local_assembly.py
+- git add src/retro_miner/local_assembly.py tests/test_local_assembly.py
+- git commit -m "feat(science): add De Bruijn micro-assembly engine for MEI breakpoint contig reconstruction"
+- git push origin HEAD
+

